# Database Modification Workflow

When modifying the MongoDB database, follow this strict sequence:

## 1. Confirm Changes First

Before writing any code, present the planned changes to the user for confirmation. Include:
- Which collection(s) will be affected
- What changes will be made (add/modify/delete fields, indexes, etc.)
- Migration strategy

```
1. Confirm: Describe the changes → wait for user approval
```

## 2. Create Seed File

After confirmation, create a temporary seed file in `api/Data/Seed/` following the existing pattern:

```csharp
namespace Ecommer.Api.Data.Seed;

public static class {Action}Seeder
{
    public static async Task SeedAsync(MongoDbContext context, ILogger logger, CancellationToken ct = default)
    {
        // Implementation
    }
}
```

File naming convention: `{Description}Seeder.cs` (e.g., `AddProductFieldSeeder.cs`, `FixUserEmailSeeder.cs`)

## 3. Apply the Seed

1. Register the seeder in `MongoStartup.cs`:
```csharp
await {Action}Seeder.SeedAsync(context, logger, ct);
```

2. Run the application to execute the seed

3. Verify the changes in MongoDB

## 4. Delete Seed File

After successful application:
1. Remove the seeder registration from `MongoStartup.cs`
2. Delete the seed file from `api/Data/Seed/`

## Seeder Pattern Reference

Seeders should be idempotent - check if changes already exist before applying:

```csharp
public static async Task SeedAsync(MongoDbContext context, ILogger logger, CancellationToken ct = default)
{
    var collection = context.CollectionName;
    
    // Check if migration already applied
    var existing = await collection.CountDocumentsAsync(filter, cancellationToken: ct);
    if (existing > 0) return;  // Already migrated
    
    // Apply changes
    await collection.UpdateManyAsync(filter, update, cancellationToken: ct);
    logger.LogInformation("Applied {Action} migration");
}
```
